## Eclipse LSP4J

`harness/daemon` directly depends on `org.eclipse.lsp4j:org.eclipse.lsp4j:1.0.0` and
`org.eclipse.lsp4j:org.eclipse.lsp4j.jsonrpc:1.0.0` for the stdio JSON-RPC transport, and pulls
`com.google.code.gson:gson:2.13.2` transitively through `org.eclipse.lsp4j.jsonrpc`.

The license statements shipped with those artifacts do not agree, and this notice does not
resolve the discrepancy:

- the Maven POMs declare `Eclipse Public License, Version 2.0`
  (`http://www.eclipse.org/legal/epl-2.0`);
- the `about.html` inside both bundled JARs states that the plug-in content is provided under the
  `Eclipse Public License Version 1.0` (`http://www.eclipse.org/legal/epl-v10.html`) and points to
  `http://www.eclipse.org` for the corresponding source. No alternative or dual-license grant
  (for example the Eclipse Distribution License) is stated in those files.

Both `about.html` files are retained inside the bundled JARs, and the
`org.eclipse.lsp4j-1.0.0-sources.jar` published alongside them carries the same
`about.html` with no separate `LICENSE` or `NOTICE` file, so the artifact metadata does not
settle the question either. A redistributor that needs one
authoritative license must establish it from the Eclipse project
(`https://github.com/eclipse-lsp4j/lsp4j`) instead of relying on this table.

